        "reasoning_content": "Let me consolidate what I know:\n\n1. `tokenize_yaml` is at `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml.tokenize_yaml`.\n2. `_get_position(content, index)` returns `Position(line_no=content.count('\\n', 0, index) + 1, column_no=index - content.rfind('\\n', 0, index), char_index=index)`.\n   - For index 0: line_no=1, column_no=0 - (-1) = 1, char_index=0. Good.\n   - For index 11 in \"key: value\\n@invalid\": line_no = count('\\n',0,11)+1 = 2, column_no = 11 - rfind('\\n',0,11)=11-10=1, char_index=11.\n3. `Token`:\n   - `value` property \u2192 `self._get_value()`\n   - `start`/`end` properties \u2192 `Position` objects (via `_get_position(self._start_index)`)\n   - `string` property \u2192 `self._content[self._start_index:self._end_index + 1]`\n   - `ScalarToken._get_value()` returns `self._value`\n   - `DictToken._get_value()` returns `{key_token._get_value(): value_token._get_value() for key_token, value_token in self._value.items()}`\n   - `ListToken._get_value()` returns `[token._get_value() for token in self._value]`\n   - `ScalarToken.__hash__` = hash(self._value)\n   - `Token.__eq__` compares only with other Tokens.\n4. `ParseError(BaseError)` \u2014 BaseError stores `_messages` list of `Message`. Constructed with `ParseError(text=..., code=..., position=...)`. So to check text/code/position, I should use `error.messages()[0].text`, `.code`, `.position`. Let me check Message attributes \u2014 Message has `text`, `code`, `key`, `position`, `index` presumably. I didn't query Message, but from BaseError.__init__: `Message(text=text, code=code, key=key, position=position)` and `message.index` is used. So Message has `.text`, `.code`, `.position`, `.index`.\n\n   Also BaseError is a Mapping \u2014 `dict(error)` gives the message dict. And `error.messages()` returns list of Message.\n\n   So for assertions:\n   - `error.messages()[0].text == \"No content.\"`\n   - `error.messages()[0].code == \"no_content\"`\n   - `error.messages()[0].position == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)` \u2014 Position has `__eq__`.\n\n   Alternatively `dict(error)` \u2192 `{'': 'No content.'}`. Hmm, the message_dict: `insert_key = message.index[-1] if message.index else ''` \u2192 `{'': 'No content.'}`. I could assert that too, but messages()[0] is cleaner.\n\n   Let me be careful: does `Message` have `.position` attribute? BaseError passes `position=position` to Message. Message likely stores it as `self.position`. I'm fairly confident. To be safe, I could assert via `error.messages()[0]` attributes text and code, and position via the message. Let me also double check `Message` \u2014 I didn't query it. Risk: attribute name might be different. But given `Message(text=text, code=code, key=key, position=position)` constructor signature, the attributes are almost certainly `text`, `code`, `key`, `position`. Also `message.index` exists.\n\n   Actually, to be safer, I could avoid relying on Message internals and instead check `dict(error)` for text: `dict(excinfo.value) == {\"\": \"No content.\"}`. Hmm, but that relies on Mapping behavior. Both are reasonable. Let me use messages()[0].text and .code \u2014 standard typesystem API. Actually, in typesystem docs, `error.messages()` returns Message objects with `.text`, `.code`, `.index`, `.position`. Yes, that's the documented API. I'll use it.\n\n5. `DictToken.__init__` does `self._child_keys = {k._value: k for k in self._value.keys()}` \u2014 keys must be Tokens with `._value`. Since our construct_mapping returns a dict whose keys are ScalarTokens (from construct_scalar etc.), that works. Note: keys must be hashable \u2014 ScalarToken defines `__hash__`. Good.\n\n   Wait \u2014 but what if a key is a plain (non-Token) value? In our flow, keys always come from our constructors, so they're Tokens. Good.\n\n6. Now the tests. Coverage targets: lines 26-109.\n\n   - Line 26 assert: any call.\n   - Bytes branch (28-29): `tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\")`.\n   - Str branch (31): `tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")`.\n   - Empty (33-36): `tokenize_yaml(\"\")` and whitespace.\n   - Constructors (38-101): exercised via parsing various docs.\n   - construct_mapping: dict doc.\n   - construct_sequence: list doc.\n   - construct_scalar: plain string doc.\n   - construct_int: \"123\".\n   - construct_float: \"1.5\".\n   - construct_bool: \"true\".\n   - construct_null: \"null\".\n   - Success return (103-104): all the above.\n   - Error path (105-109): invalid YAML like \"@invalid\".\n\n   For the error path: `yaml.load(\"@invalid\", ...)` raises ScannerError with problem \"found character '@' that cannot start any token\", problem_mark.index == 0. So text = \"found character '@' that cannot start any token.\" and position = Position(1, 1, 0).\n\n   Let me double check the problem message for \"@\": PyYAML scanner: `found character '%r' that cannot start any token` \u2014 actually the message is:\n\n   ```\n   while scanning for the next token\n   found character '@' that cannot start any token\n   ```\n\n   The `problem` attribute is \"found character '@' that cannot start any token\". Yes. So text = \"found character '@' that cannot start any token.\"\n\n   I could assert the exact text. It's stable across PyYAML versions (this message has been there forever). I'll assert exact text but... hmm, to be a bit safe I might assert `endswith(\".\")` plus contains \"@\". Actually let me assert exact \u2014 it's well-known stable. Hmm, but if I'm wrong the test fails. Let me think again: yaml/scanner.py:\n\n   ```python\n   if ch in '\\'@':\n       raise ScannerError(\"while scanning for the next token\", start_mark, ...\n   ```\n   \n   Actually the code:\n   ```python\n   # Check if we really have the directive...\n   if ch == '@':\n       ...\n   ```\n   The exact snippet:\n   ```python\n   else:\n       raise ScannerError(\"while scanning for the next token\", start_mark,\n               \"found character %r that cannot start any token\" % ch, self.get_m