h line_no > 1:\n   `tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\\n@invalid\")` \u2192 problem_mark.index == 11 \u2192 Position(line_no=2, column_no=1, char_index=11).\n\n   Wait, let me double-check rfind: `content.rfind('\\n', 0, 11)` \u2014 content = \"key: value\\n@invalid\", indices: k(0)e(1)y(2):(3) (4)v(5)a(6)l(7)u(8)e(9)\\n(10)@(11). rfind('\\n', 0, 11) searches in content[0:11] = \"key: value\\n\" \u2192 finds at index 10. column_no = 11 - 10 = 1. line_no = count('\\n',0,11)+1 = 1+1 = 2. char_index = 11. \n\n   But is problem_mark.index exactly 11? The scanner scans tokens: \"key\" scalar, \":\", \"value\" scalar, then next token starts at index 11 where '@' is found. The error mark is `self.get_mark()` at the current position, which is index 11. Yes.\n\n   Hmm, one caveat: after scanning \"value\" scalar, the scanner might consume the newline as part of token separation. The '@' check happens when fetching the next token; the mark is at the '@' position (index 11). Yes.\n\n7. Token assertions:\n   - For `tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")`: DictToken with `_value` = {ScalarToken(\"key\",0,2): ScalarToken(\"value\",5,9)}. `token.value` \u2192 `{\"key\": \"value\"}`. `token.string` \u2192 content[0:10] = \"key: value\". `token.start` \u2192 Position for index 0 \u2192 (1,1,0). `token.end` \u2192 Position for index 9 \u2192 line_no=1, column_no=10, char_index=9.\n\n   Wait \u2014 `Token._get_position(self, index)` \u2014 I didn't get its body, but it presumably calls the module-level `_get_position(self._content, index)`. So `token.end` = Position(line_no=1, column_no=10, char_index=9). Let me verify: _get_position(content, 9): count('\\n',0,9)=0 \u2192 line_no=1; rfind('\\n',0,9) = -1 \u2192 column_no = 9 - (-1) = 10; char_index=9. Yes.\n\n   Hmm, but asserting on `token.end` Position requires knowing `_get_position` method behavior \u2014 it's presumably the same. I could assert `token.end.char_index == 9` which is safe regardless of line/column computation. Let me assert char_index only for start/end, plus full Position equality for the error positions (where I know the module-level function).\n\n   Actually for safety, let me assert `token.start.char_index == 0` and `token.end.char_index == 9`. And maybe line_no/column_no too since I know the formula: start \u2192 (1,1,0); end \u2192 (1,10,9). I'm confident in the module-level `_get_position`; `Token._get_position` presumably delegates. To be safe, assert char_index and line_no (line_no = count of newlines + 1 = 1 for single-line). Column: 10. I'll include them but... risk if Token._get_position differs. Let me just assert char_index for token start/end, and full positions for ParseError (module-level function confirmed).\n\n   Hmm, actually let me include line_no too \u2014 for single-line content, line_no=1 under any sane implementation. Column_no I'll skip for tokens. Actually, let me keep it simple: assert char_index only.\n\n   - For `tokenize_yaml(\"- a\\n- b\")`: ListToken, value = [\"a\", \"b\"], string = \"- a\\n- b\" (start=0, end_mark.index=7 \u2192 end=6 \u2192 content[0:7]). Good.\n\n   - For \"hello\": ScalarToken, value=\"hello\", string=\"hello\", start char_index 0, end char_index 4.\n\n   - For \"123\": value == 123. Note: `token.value == 123` \u2014 Token.__eq__ only works with other Tokens, but here I'm comparing `token.value` (an int) with 123. Fine.\n\n   - For \"1.5\": value == 1.5.\n   - For \"true\": value is True.\n   - For \"null\": value is None.\n\n   Also nested test to exercise recursion: `tokenize_yaml(\"a: [1, 2]\")` \u2192 DictToken.value == {\"a\": [1, 2]}. DictToken._get_value calls key/value `_get_value()`; value is ListToken \u2192 its `_get_value` converts items. Good. This exercises construct_mapping + construct_sequence + construct_int in one doc. But I already have separate tests. I can add a combined nested test for good measure.\n\n   Also a dict with mixed types: `tokenize_yaml(\"string: hello\\nint: 123\\nfloat: 1.5\\nbool: true\\nnull: null\"