ml_constructors.copy()\n    cls.yaml_constructors[tag] = constructor\n```\n\nCustomSafeLoader is a fresh class each call; 'yaml_constructors' not in its __dict__ \u2192 copies SafeLoader's (via MRO) into CustomSafeLoader.__dict__. SafeLoader untouched. \u2713 No pollution.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 one thing: `yaml_constructors` copy \u2014 SafeLoader.yaml_constructors includes constructors for bool/int/float/null/str/etc. Our overrides replace them on the copy. Fine.\n\nSo no monkeypatch needed. I'll note in a comment that the loader subclass is function-local so no global YAML state is mutated.\n\nNow, the final test file. Also consider: should tests assert `isinstance(token, Token)`? Fine as is.\n\nLet me also think about whether to test `tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")` returning token whose `.value` keys are plain strings \u2014 done.\n\nOne more: the assert on line 26 \u2014 covered by every test.\n\nLet me also double check the import of Position: `from typesystem.base import ParseError, Position`. \u2713 (module imports it too).\n\nEdge: `pytest.importorskip(\"yaml\")` \u2014 returns the yaml module; fine as a bare call.\n\nNow, is there any issue with `excinfo.value.messages()[0]`? ParseError inherits BaseError; messages() returns [Message]. \u2713.\n\nLet me also add an assertion using `dict(error)` for the no_content case as a cross-check? Maybe: `assert dict(excinfo.value) == {\"\": \"No content.\"}`. Hmm, BaseError.__getitem__ returns `self._message_dict[key]`... and __iter__ iterates over _message_dict keys. So dict(error) = {'': 'No content.'}. I'm fairly confident. But it adds risk; the messages() approach is enough. I'll include just messages().\n\nActually, let me include both text/code/position via message. Good enough.\n\nFinal check on test for parse error text: \"found character that cannot start any token.\" \u2014 with the period appended. Let me write `assert message.text == \"found character that cannot start any token.\"`.\n\nHmm, one lingering doub