Then write the test.\n\nFor coverage of lines 26-109:\n- Line 26: assert - executed whenever function is called\n- Lines 28-29: bytes branch - test with bytes\n- Line 31: str branch - test with str\n- Lines 33-36: empty content - test with \"\" and \"   \"\n- Lines 38-101: constructor setup - executed on any non-empty parse\n- Line 41-44: construct_mapping - test with mapping\n- Line 47-51: construct_sequence - test with sequence\n- Line 53-57: construct_scalar - test with plain string scalar\n- Line 59-63: construct_int - test with int\n- Line 65-69: construct_float - test with float\n- Line 71-75: construct_bool - test with bool\n- Line 77-81: construct_null - test with null\n- Line 103-104: successful load\n- Line 105-109: parse error handling\n\nNote: for nested structures, e.g. a dict containing an int, both construct_mapping and construct_int get called. But to be safe, test each type at top level.\n\nWait, there's a subtlety: for a plain scalar like \"hello\", the resolver resolves it to tag `tag:yaml.org,2002:str` which is DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG. So construct_scalar is called. For \"123\", the resolver resolves to `tag:yaml.org,2002:int`, so construct_int is called. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety with `construct_scalar` for the str tag: `loader.construct_scalar(node)` returns the string value. Good.\n\nFor null: \"null\" resolves to `tag:yaml.org,2002:null`, construct_null calls `loader.construct_yaml_null(node)` which returns None. Good.\n\nFor bool: \"true\" -> construct_yaml_bool -> True.\n\nFor float: \"1.5\" -> construct_yaml_float -> 1.5.\n\nNow, the tokens:\n- ScalarToken(value, start, end - 1, content=str_content)\n\nFor \"123\", start=0, end=3 (end_mark.index is 3), so end-1=2. The token's `string` would be content[0:3]? Let me check the Token class. In typesystem, `Token.__init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None)` and `self._string = content[start_index:end_index]` or something. Let me check with get_info.\n\nActually let 