em repo (encode/typesystem), tokenize/tokens.py:\n\n```python\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        value: typing.Any,\n        start_index: int,\n        end_index: int,\n        content: str = \"\",\n    ) -> None:\n        self.value = value\n        self.start = start_index\n        self.end = end_index\n        self.content = content\n\n    @property\n    def string(self) -> str:\n        return self.content[self.start : self.end + 1]\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        ...\n    def __repr__(self):\n        ...\n```\n\nAnd DictToken:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n\n    def _get_value(self, item):\n        ...\n\n    @property\n    def value(self):\n        return {self._get_value(key): self._get_value(value) for key, value in self.value...}\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure. Let me just use get_info.\n\nActually wait \u2014 in the tokenize_yaml code, `DictToken(mapping, start, end - 1, content=str_content)` passes the raw mapping (a dict of raw Python values) as `value`. Then DictToken's `value` property might convert keys/values into tokens... Actually no. Let me look at the real code:\n\nFrom typesystem/tokenize/tokens.py (real source):\n\n```python\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        value: typing.Any,\n        start_index: int,\n        end_index: int,\n        content: str = \"\",\n    ) -> None:\n        self.value = value\n        self.start = start_index\n        self.end = end_index\n        self.content = content\n\n    @property\n    def string(self) -> str:\n        return self.content[self.start : self.end + 1]\n\n    def __eq__(self, other: typing.Any) -> bool:\n        return self.value == other.value\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        class_name = self.__class__.__name__\n        arg = repr(self.value)\n        if self.string != self.value:\n            arg += \", \" + repr(self.string)\n        return f\"{class_name}({arg})\"\n\n\nclass ScalarToken(Token):\n    def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n\n    def get_value(self):\n        return self.value\n\n\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n\n    def _get_value(self, item):\n        if isinstance(item, Token):\n            return item.get_value()\n        return item\n\n    def get_value(self):\n        return {\n            self._get_value(key): self._get_value(value)\n            for key, value in self.value.items()\n        }\n\n\nclass ListToken(Token):\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure. The important thing for the test: `tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")` returns a DictToken whose `.value` is the parsed dict `{\"key\": \"value\"}` (raw values, since construct_mapping returns `loader.construct_mapping(node)` which is a plain dict). Actually wait, but nested values would be Tokens because the constructors return Tokens... Hmm, no. `loader.construct_mapping(node)` calls the registered constructors for child nodes, which are our custom ones returning Tokens. So the mapping dict would map key Tokens to value Tokens? Hmm, wait.\n\nActually, `loader.construct_mapping(node)` \u2014 the SafeConstructor.construct_mapping calls construct_object for each key and value, which uses the registered constructors. So for `key: value`, the key node is a scalar with str tag -> construct_scalar -> ScalarToken(\"key\", ...), and value node -> ScalarToken(\"value\", ...). So `mapping` = {ScalarToken(\"key\"): ScalarToken(\"value\")}.\n\nHmm wait, but then DictToken.value would be a dict with Token keys. And DictToken.get_value() converts them.\n\nHmm, but actually in the real typesystem code, I recall:\n\n```python\ndef construct_mapping(loader, node):\n    start = node.start_mark.index\n    end = node.end_mark.index\n    mapping = loader.construct_mapping(node)\n    return DictToken(mapping, start, end - 1, content=str_content)\n```\n\nAnd DictToken:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def get_value(self):\n        return {\n            key.get_value() if isinstance(key, Token) else key: (\n                value.get_value() if isinstance(value, Token) else value\n            )\n            for key, value in self.value.items()\n        }\n```\n\nHmm. Actually let me look at the actual typesystem source. I'll fetch via get_info.\n\nLet me also check `_get_position`:\n\n```python\ndef _get_position(content: str, index: int) -> Position:\n    if index == 0:\n        return Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n    ...\n```\n\nSomething like that. Let me check.\n\nOK, plan: call get_info for:\n1. `_get_position`\n2. `Token`\n3. `DictToken`\n4. `ListToken`\n5. `ScalarToken`\n6. `ParseError`\n7. `Position`\n\nThese are independent, so I can call them all in one block.\n\nThen write the test file.\n\nThe test file:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}  # or token.value\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_str():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    ...\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_empty_string():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"\")\n    assert exc_info.value.text == \"No content.\"\n    assert exc_info.value.code == \"no_content\"\n    position = exc_info.value.position\n    assert position.line_no == 1\n    assert position.column_no == 1\n    assert position.char_index == 0\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_whitespace():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"   \\n  \")\n    assert exc_info.value.code == \"no_content\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_mapping():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n    assert token.string == \"key: value\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_sequence():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"- 1\\n- 2\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ListToken)\n    assert token.get_value() == [1, 2]\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"hello\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == \"hello\"\n    assert token.string == \"hello\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_int():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"123\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 123\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_float():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"1.5\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 1.5\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bool():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"true\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is True\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_null():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"null\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is None\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_parse_error():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"@invalid\")\n    assert exc_info.value.code == \"parse_error\"\n    assert exc_info.value.text.endswith(\".\")\n    assert exc_info.value.position is not None\n```\n\nWait \u2014 for the parse error test, I need to double check what `exc.problem` is for `@invalid`. The ScannerError for \"@\" character: problem = \"found character '@' that cannot start any token\". So text = \"found character '@' that cannot start any token.\" Good.\n\nAnd `_get_position(str_content, index=exc.problem_mark.index)`. For \"@invalid\", problem_mark.index = 0. Let me check what _get_position does with index 0.\n\nFrom typesystem source (tokenize/tokenize_yaml.py):\n\n```python\ndef _get_position(content: str, index: int) -> Position:\n    if index == 0:\n        return Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n\n    line_no = content.count(\"\\n\", 0, index) + 1\n    if line_no > 1:\n        last_line_break_index = content.rindex(\"\\n\", 0, index)\n        column_no = index - last_line_break_index\n    else:\n        column_no = index + 1\n\n    return Position(line_no=line_no, column_no=column_no, char_index=index)\n```\n\nSomething like that. So for index 0, position = (1, 1, 0).\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 for \"@invalid\", is problem_mark.index really 0? The scanner error occurs when scanning the token starting at index 0. Yes, problem_mark should be at index 0.\n\nActually, let me double-check: `yaml.load(\"@invalid\", SafeLoader)`:\n\n```\nyaml.scanner.ScannerError: while scanning for the next token\nfound character '@' that cannot start any token\n  in \"<unicode string>\", line 1, column 1\n```\n\nThe problem_mark is at line 1, column 1, index 0. Yes.\n\nAlternatively, I could use a multi-line error to exercise the line_no > 1 branch of _get_position, but that's not required for coverage of lines 26-109 (the _get_position function is presumably in a different line range or already covered). But it doesn't hurt. Actually, the task says lines 26-109 don't execute. _get_position is probably defined before line 26 (lines 1-25 or so). So I don't need to cover its branches, but testing with a multi-line document would be nice anyway. Let me keep it simple but maybe add one test with an error on a later line to be thorough. Actually, let me not overcomplicate; but a second parse error test with content on line 2 could be good for robustness. Hmm, the instruction says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\". Branches within lines 26-109:\n\n- Line 28 `if isinstance(content, bytes)`: both branches (bytes test + str test)\n- Line 33 `if not str_content.strip()`: both branches (empty test + non-empty tests)\n- Line 103 try/except: both branches (success tests + error test)\n\nThat's it for branches within the function. The nested functions' bodies are executed when the constructors are called during yaml.load.\n\nNow, about the token API. Let me verify with get_info what DictToken etc. look like. Let me make the calls.\n\nAlso, I need to be careful about the import path. The file is `typesystem/tokenize/tokenize_yaml.py`. The function might be importable as `from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml` or `from typesystem.tokenize import tokenize_yaml` (if re-exported). Let me check with get_info on \"typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml.tokenize_yaml\" or just \"tokenize_yaml\".\n\nLet me also check whether there's a `yaml` import guard: `import yaml` at the top \u2014 if pyyaml isn't installed, the module import fails. But we assume it's installed since the code exists.\n\nAlso note: the module-level code might have a try/except around `import yaml`:\n\nActually in the real typesystem source:\n\n```python\nimport typing\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\n\ntry:\n    import yaml\n    from yaml.loader import SafeLoader\nexcept ImportError:  # pragma: no cover\n    yaml = None  # type: ignore\n```\n\nHmm, but the given code shows plain imports. The assert on line 26 `assert yaml is not None, \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\"` suggests yaml could be None. But the given snippet shows `import yaml` directly. Either way, the assert line is covered by any call.\n\nNow, about state pollution: `CustomSafeLoader` is defined inside the function, and `add_constructor` is called on the subclass, not on SafeLoader itself. Wait \u2014 `CustomSafeLoader.add_constructor(...)` \u2014 add_constructor is a classmethod on BaseResolver that modifies `yaml_constructors` dict. Since CustomSafeLoader is a fresh subclass defined per call, `add_constructor` on it... hmm, actually `BaseResolver.add_constructor` is:\n\n```python\n@classmethod\ndef add_constructor(cls, tag, constructor):\n    if not 'yaml_constructors' in cls.__dict__:\n        cls.yaml_constructors = cls.yaml_constructors.copy()\n    cls.yaml_constructors[tag] = constructor\n```\n\nSo it copies the dict into the subclass's own `__dict__` \u2014 no pollution of SafeLoader. Good, no state pollution there. Each call to tokenize_yaml defines a new CustomSafeLoader class. So no cleanup needed for that.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is there any global state? `yaml.resolver.BaseResolver.add_constructor` \u2014 since CustomSafeLoader doesn't have 'yaml_constructors' in its own `__dict__` initially, it copies from... hmm, `cls.yaml_constructors` resolves via MRO to BaseResolver's or SafeLoader's. Then it sets `cls.yaml_constructors = copy` on CustomSafeLoader. So SafeLoader is untouched. \n\nHowever, there's a subtlety: if `tokenize_yaml` is called multiple times, each creates a new class. No pollution.\n\nBut to be safe about state pollution, I could monkeypatch nothing. The tests are pure \u2014 they don't modify global state. The only concern: importing the module requires pyyaml. Fine.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `token.value` for DictToken is the raw dict with Token keys/values, and whether `get_value()` exists. Let me check the actual typesystem source.\n\nReal typesystem tokenize/tokens.py (from encode/typesystem repo):\n\n```python\nimport re\nimport typing\n\nfrom typesystem.base import Position\n\n\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        value: typing.Any,\n        start_index: int,\n        end_index: int,\n        content: str = \"\",\n    ) -> None:\n        self.value = value\n        self.start = start_index\n        self.end = end_index\n        self.content = content\n\n    @property\n    def string(self) -> str:\n        return self.content[self.start : self.end + 1]\n\n    def __eq__(self, other: typing.Any) -> bool:\n        if isinstance(other, Token):\n            return (\n                self.value == 