essages()[0] primarily.\n\n    Hmm, actually let me reconsider: `error.messages()` \u2014 BaseError.messages(add_prefix=None) returns `self._messages` (possibly prefixed). So `error.messages()[0]` is the Message. Good.\n\n14. One more: for the parse error test, `error.messages()[0].position` \u2014 Message.position. And `error.messages()[0].index` \u2014 probably `[position.char_index]` or similar. I'll skip index.\n\nNow, let me also double check `DictToken.__init__`: `self._child_keys = {k._value: k for k in self._value.keys()}`. For `tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")`, keys are ScalarTokens \u2192 fine.\n\nBut wait \u2014 what about `tokenize_yaml(\"hello\")`? That's a ScalarToken root; no DictToken involved. Fine.\n\nWhat about the sequence test \"- a\\n- b\": ListToken._value = [ScalarToken(\"a\"), ScalarToken(\"b\")]. value \u2192 [\"a\", \"b\"]. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one thing to double check: for the root scalar \"hello\", `construct_scalar` is registered for DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG. But wait, there's a catch: PyYAML's SafeConstructor has `construct_yaml_str` etc. registered for specific tags, and the resolver resolves plain scalars to specific tags (str/int/float/bool/null/timestamp/etc.), NOT to DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG unless unresolved. Hmm! Wait, this is important.\n\n`yaml.resolver.BaseResolver.DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG = 'tag:yaml.org,2002:str'`. The resolver's implicit resolvers map plain scalars to specific tags: str, bool, int, float, timestamp, null, merge... For \"hello\", the resolved tag is 'tag:yaml.org,2002:str' which IS DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG. So `add_constructor(DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG, construct_scalar)` overrides `construct_yaml_str`. Good \u2014 construct_scalar is called for strings.\n\nFor \"123\" \u2192 'tag:yaml.org,2002:int' \u2192 construct_int overrides construct_yaml_int. Good.\n\nFor \"1.5\" \u2192 float tag \u2192 construct_float. Good.\n\nFor \"true\" \u2192 bool tag \u2192 construct_bool. Good.\n\nFor \"null\" \u2192 null tag \u2192 construct_null. Good.\n\nFor quoted scalars like `\"hello\"` \u2192 tag would be DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG (str) as well.\n\nWhat about timestamps? \"2021-01-01\" \u2192 timestamp tag \u2192 SafeConstructor.construct_timestamp \u2014 not overridden, returns a datetime.date, not a ScalarToken. Then if nested in a dict, DictToken._get_value would call `._get_value()` on it \u2192 AttributeError. But I'm not testing timestamps. Avoid.\n\nWhat about \"yes\"/\"no\"? In PyYAML's default resolver (YAML 1.1), \"yes\" \u2192 bool. Not needed.\n\nOK now, one more potential gotcha: for the root document, `yaml.load` returns whatever the constructor returns \u2014 our Token. Good.\n\nAnother gotcha: `construct_mapping` calls `loader.construct_mapping(node)` \u2014 SafeConstructor.construct_mapping flattens merge keys etc. Fine.\n\nNow, about `DictToken._child_keys` \u2014 `{k._value: k ...}` \u2014 for the mapping test, k is ScalarToken(\"key\") \u2192 k._value = \"key\". Fine.\n\nEdge: duplicate keys? Not tested.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `token.value` for DictToken returns plain dict: `DictToken._get_value` \u2192 `{key_token._get_value(): value_token._get_value() ...}` \u2192 {\"key\": \"value\"}. \n\nNow write the tests:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\npytest.importorskip(\"yaml\")\n\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\n```\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 importorskip before imports: E402 style but functionally needed. Actually, if pyyaml is installed, order doesn't matter. But logically importorskip must run before importing the module under test. Fine.\n\nActually, hmm, should I include importorskip at all? The environment testing this code must have pyyaml (the module imports it unconditionally per the snippet). If pyyaml were missing, the module import itself fails and there's nothing to test. importorskip just makes it graceful. I'll include it \u2014 it's one line and harmless.\n\nHmm, but wait: if the module under test has `import yaml` unguarded and pyyaml is missing, then `from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml` raises ImportError at collection \u2014 importorskip placed before it prevents that by skipping the module first. OK include.\n\nTests:\n\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_input():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n    assert token.string == \"key: value\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_input_with_invalid_utf8_byte_is_ignored():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\\xff\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n    assert token.string == \"key: value\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_str_input():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n    assert token.string == \"key: value\"\n    assert token.start.char_index == 0\n    assert token.end.char_index == len(\"key: value\") - 1\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `token.value == {\"key\": \"value\"}`: token.value is a dict \u2192 plain dict comparison. Good.\n\n`token.start` is a Position; `.char_index` == 0. `token.end.char_index` == 9. Good.\n\nEmpty content:\n\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_empty_string_raises_no_content_error():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as excinfo:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"\")\n    message = excinfo.value.messages()[0]\n    assert message.text == \"No content.\"\n    assert message.code == \"no_content\"\n    assert message.position == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_whitespace_only_string_raises_no_content_error():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as excinfo:\n        tokenize_yaml(\" \\n\\t \")\n    assert excinfo.value.messages()[0].code == \"no_content\"\n```\n\nMapping/sequence/scalars:\n\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_mapping():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_nested_mapping_and_sequence():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"a: [1, 2]\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"a\": [1, 2]}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_sequence():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"- a\\n- b\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ListToken)\n    assert token.value == [\"a\", \"b\"]\n    assert token.string == \"- a\\n- b\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_string_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"hello\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == \"hello\"\n    assert token.string == \"hello\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_int_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"123\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 123\n    assert token.string == \"123\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_float_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"1.5\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 1.5\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bool_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"true\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is True\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_null_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"null\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is None\n```\n\nParse errors:\n\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_parse_error_on_first_line():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as excinfo:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"@invalid\")\n    message = excinfo.value.messages()[0]\n    assert message.code == \"parse_error\"\n    assert message.text == \"found character '@' that cannot start any token.\"\n    assert message.position == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_parse_error_on_later_line():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as excinfo:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\\n@invalid\")\n    message = excinfo.value.messages()[0]\n    assert message.code == \"parse_error\"\n    assert message.position == Position(line_no=2, column_no=1, char_index=11)\n```\n\nHmm, for the second one, should I assert the text too? Same problem message. I'll assert code and position; text same as before \u2014 could assert `message.text == \"found character '@' that cannot start any token.\"` too. Fine, include.\n\nWait, one concern on the exact problem text: is it \"found character '@' that cannot start any token\"? Let me re-derive from PyYAML source (scanner.py, fetch_more_tokens \u2192 fetch_next_token):\n\n```python\n            # No? It's an error. Let's produce a nice error message.\n            # We try to guess what the user might have intended...\n            if ch in '\\'':\n                ...\n            elif ch in '%@':\n                ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually the relevant code:\n\n```python\n        else:\n            raise ScannerError(\"while scanning for the next token\", start_mark,\n            \"found character %r that cannot start any token\"\n            % ch, self.get_mark())\n```\n\nHmm wait, actually I recall there's special handling:\n\n```python\n    def fetch_more_tokens(self):\n        ...\n    def fetch_next_token(self):\n        ...\n        # Check the beginning of the token\n        if ch == '%' and self.peek... \n```\n\nThe actual code in fetch_next_token:\n\n```python\n        # Is it the flow sequence start indicator?\n        if ch == '[':\n            return self.fetch_flow_sequence_start()\n        ...\n        # Is it a plain scalar?\n        ...\n        # No? It's an error. Let's produce a nice error message.\n        # We try to guess what the user might have intended...\n        if ch in '\\'\"':\n            ...\n        if ch in '\\t ':\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm, I remember there's something like:\n\n```python\n        if ch == '\\t':\n            ...\n```\n\nActually the exact snippet from PyYAML scanner.py fetch_next_token end:\n\n```python\n        # No? It's an error. Let's produce a nice error message.\n        # We try to guess what the user might have intended...\n        if ch in '\\'':\n            ...\n```\n\nI don't remember exactly, but the final fallback is:\n\n```python\n            raise ScannerError(\"while scanning for the next token\", start_mark,\n                    \"found character %r that cannot start any token\"\n                    % ch, self.get_mark())\n```\n\nHmm wait, actually I think there's a special case for '@' and '`':\n\n```python\n        if ch in '@`':\n            ...\n```\n\nNo wait. Let me recall the real code:\n\n```python\n    def fetch_next_token(self):\n\n        # Peek ahead to the next character...\n        self.scan_to_next_token()\n\n        # Remove obsolete possible simple keys.\n        self.stale_possible_simple_keys()\n\n        # Compare the current indentation level...\n        self.unroll_indent(self.column)\n\n        # Peek the next character.\n        ch = self.peek()\n\n        # Is it the end of stream?\n        if ch is None:\n            return self.fetch_stream_end()\n\n        # Is it a directive?\n        if ch == '%':\n            return self.fetch_directive()\n\n        # Is it the document start?\n        if ch == '-' and self.peek(1) == '-' and self.peek(2) == '-':\n            return self.fetch_document_indicator(DocumentStartToken)\n\n        # Is it the document end?\n        if ch == '.' and self.peek(1) == '.' and self.peek(2) == '.':\n            return self.fetch_document_indicator(DocumentEndToken)\n\n        # Is it the flow sequence start indicator?\n        if ch == '[':\n            return self.fetch_flow_sequence_start()\n\n        # Is it the flow mapping start indicator?\n        if ch == '{':\n            return self.fetch_flow_mapping_start()\n\n        # Is it the flow sequence end indicator?\n        if ch == ']':\n            return self.fetch_flow_sequence_end()\n\n        # Is it the flow mapping end indicator?\n        if ch == '}':\n            return self.fetch_flow_mapping_end()\n\n        # Is it the flow entry indicator?\n        if ch == ',':\n            return self.fetch_flow_entry()\n\n        # Is it the block entry indicator?\n        if ch == '-':\n            return self.fetch_block_entry()\n\n        # Is it the key indicator?\n        if ch == '?':\n            return self.fetch_key()\n\n        # Is it the value indicator?\n        if ch == ':':\n            return self.fetch_value()\n\n        # Is it an alias?\n        if ch == '*':\n            return self.fetch_alias()\n\n        # Is it an anchor?\n        if ch == '&':\n            return self.fetch_anchor()\n\n        # Is it a tag?\n        if ch == '!':\n            return self.fetch_tag()\n\n        # Is it a literal scalar?\n        if ch == '|':\n            return self.fetch_literal()\n\n        # Is it a folded scalar?\n        if ch == '>':\n            return self.fetch_folded()\n\n        # Is it a single quoted scalar?\n        if ch == '\\'':\n            return self.fetch_single()\n\n        # Is it a double quoted scalar?\n        if ch == '\"':\n            return self.fetch_double()\n\n        # It must be a plain scalar if it's not...\n        if not (ch is None or ch in '\\t \\r\\n\\x85\\u2028\\u2029-:,[]{}#&*!|>\\'\"%`@'):\n            return self.fetch_plain()\n\n        # If it's... hmm.\n```\n\nHmm, actually the check is:\n\n```python\n        # It must be a plain scalar if it's not...\n        if ch not in '-:,[]{}#&*!|>\\'\"%`@\\t \\r\\n\\x85\\u2028\\u2029':\n            return self.fetch_plain()\n```\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 '@' IS in that exclusion list, so '@' does NOT start a plain scalar, and falls through to:\n\n```python\n        # If we don't determine the token type so far, it is an error.\n        raise ScannerError(\"while scanning for the next token\", self.get_mark(),\n                \"found character that cannot start any token\",\n                self.get_mark())\n```\n\nHmm! Is the message \"found character that cannot start any token\" (without the character) or with `%r`? Let me recall... I believe modern PyYAML says:\n\n```\nfound character that cannot start any token\n```\n\nHmm. Let me think. I've seen this error message many times:\n\n```\nyaml.scanner.ScannerError: while scanning for the next token\nfound character that cannot start any token\n  in \"<unicode string>\", line 1, column 1\n```\n\nYes! I'm now fairly sure the message is \"found character that cannot start any token\" WITHOUT the character repr in recent PyYAML. Hmm, but older versions? Let me think...\n\nActually, checking PyYAML 5.x/6.x scanner.py:\n\n```python\n        # No? It's an error. Let's produce a nice error message.\n        # We try to guess what the user might have intended...\n        if ch in '\\'':\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me recall the actual tail of fetch_next_token:\n\n```python\n        # It must be a plain scalar if it's not...\n        if ch not in '-:,[]{}#&*!|>\\'\"%`@\\t \\r\\n\\x85\\u2028\\u2029':\n            return self.fetch_plain()\n\n        # If we don't determine the token type so far, it is an error.\n        raise ScannerError(\"while scanning for the next token\", self.get_mark(),\n                \"found character that cannot start any token\",\n                self.get_mark())\n```\n\nYes \u2014 I'm now quite confident the message is \"found character that cannot start any token\" (no character mentioned). I've definitely seen that exact message in tracebacks.\n\nSo for \"@invalid\": problem = \"found character that cannot start any token\", text = \"found character that cannot start any token.\"\n\nHmm OK so my earlier guess with '%r' was wrong. The safe assertion: `message.text == \"found character that cannot start any token.\"` \u2014 I'm fairly confident. But to be extra safe, I could assert `message.text.endswith(\".\")` and `message.code == \"parse_error\"`. Hmm.\n\nThe instruction says \"Be sure to include assertions in the test that verify any applicable postconditions.\" I want meaningful assertions. Let me assert:\n\n```python\nassert message.code == \"parse_error\"\nassert message.text == \"found character that cannot start any token.\"\n```\n\nI'm quite confident about this message in PyYAML (it's been stable for many years \u2014 I recall it verbatim from many tracebacks). Let me go with it. Actually, hmm, let me reconsider once more. Searching memory: \"yaml.scanner.ScannerError: while scanning for the next token found character that cannot start any token\" \u2014 yes, this is the canonical message. E.g., when a YAML file contains a line starting with '@'. Confirmed.\n\nAnd problem_mark for this error: `self.get_mark()` at the current position \u2014 for \"@invalid\" at index 0. Both start_mark and problem_mark are the same mark here. index=0. Good.\n\nFor the multi-line case \"key: value\\n@invalid\": after scanning \"value\" plain scalar... wait, actually, hmm: \"key: value\\n@invalid\" \u2014 the scanner scans \"key\", \":\", \"value\", then scan_to_next_token skips the newline, position at index 11 ('@'). fetch_next_token: ch='@' \u2192 falls through \u2192 ScannerError with mark at index 11. Good.\n\nHmm wait, one more check: is '@' really excluded from plain scalar start chars? The exclusion string: `-:,[]{}#&*!|>'\"%`@\\t \\r\\n\\x85\\u2028\\u2029`. Yes, '@' is in it (also '`'). So '@' cannot start a token \u2192 error. Good.\n\nAlternatively, I could use a tab-indented doc: \"\\tkey: value\" \u2192 ScannerError \"found character '\\\\t' that cannot start any token\"? For tab: ch='\\t' is in the exclusion list too \u2192 same generic message. OK.\n\nOr unclosed flow: \"{foo\" \u2192 ParserError. The parser error: \"while parsing a flow mapping\" context, problem \"expected ',' or '}', but got '<stream end>'\"? Hmm, for \"{foo\": after key \"foo\" in flow mapping, expects ',' or '}' but finds stream end. Actually wait \u2014 in flow mapping, after 